# LectureSeek

LectureSeek is a proposed learning platform that helps students study recorded lectures and helps instructors prepare lecture resources. The project explores connecting video playback with transcripts, slides, chapters, summaries, quizzes, translation, dubbed audio, and personal study tools.

> **Project status:** This repository is currently a requirements and architecture documentation workspace. It does not contain a complete, runnable LectureSeek application. Capabilities described here are proposed or specified behavior unless a document explicitly reports an implemented experiment.

## Start here

For a quick introduction, read these in order:

1. [Problem definition](docs/requirements/lectureseek-problem-definition.docx) — the problem, users, and project goals.
2. [Software Requirements Specification](<LectureSeek Software Requirements Specification Draft.docx>) — functional requirements, constraints, and open decisions.
3. [Revised user stories](<LectureSeek User Stories Revised.docx>) — expected behavior and acceptance criteria for Students and Instructors.
4. [Confirmed high-level use-case analysis](<LectureSeek_Use_Case_Analysis_Confirmed_Actors.docx>) — actors, 32 high-level use cases (HL-01 to HL-32), traceability, and modeling decisions.

The `docs/requirements/` folder also contains versioned copies of the initial requirements deliverables.

## Architecture and design

- [Level Zero architecture source](<LectureSeek Architecture Diagram level-0 0.1 - revised.drawio>) — system boundary, actors, interfaces, and external systems.
- [Level One architecture source](<LectureSeek Architecture Diagram level-1 0.2.drawio>) — the Level Zero view expanded with proposed internal modules and their connections.
- [Module design and responsibilities](<LectureSeek Level One Module Design.docx>) — proposed module boundaries, responsibilities, inputs, outputs, and AI handoffs.
- [Module to high-level use-case mapping](<LectureSeek Module High-Level Functions.docx>) — maps HL-01 to HL-32 to the proposed modules.
- [Interface and high-level use-case grouping](<LectureSeek Interfaces and High Level Use Cases.docx>) — an earlier grouping of use cases under proposed interfaces.
- [Architecture planning notes](architecture_diagram_plan.md) — scope and decisions for the system-level diagram.

Open `.drawio` files with [diagrams.net](https://app.diagrams.net/) to view or edit them. The Level One modules are architectural proposals, not confirmed software services.

### Proposed internal modules

The Level One design groups capabilities into ten modules:

1. Account & Access
2. Lecture Library
3. Transcript Processing
4. Slide & Visual Processing
5. Alignment & Chapters
6. Search & Playback
7. Learning Content
8. Study & Assessment
9. Translation & Dubbing
10. Discussion & Issue Reporting

The module-to-use-case document is the detailed mapping reference. High-level functions remain identified by their `HL-` numbers so they can be traced to the use-case analysis.

### External services

The architecture currently shows four external service categories: a video source platform, a media-processing service, a supported LLM service, and a translation service. The exact providers, APIs, deployment boundaries, and final integration contracts still need to be agreed. They should be treated as design dependencies, not as integrations that are already operational.

## Feature and AI design reports

These reports provide design context for proposed capabilities:

- [Visual processing report](<LectureSeek Visual Processing Report Revised.docx>) — slide detection, OCR, timestamps, and visual-content handling.
- [Quiz generation design](<LectureSeek_Quiz_Generation_Design_Revised.docx>) — quiz generation, review, and answer evaluation.
- [Dubbing report](<LectureSeek Dubbing Revised.docx>) — translated speech, audio timing, and playback behavior.
- [Original project document set](<LecureSeek-Web-App/README.md>) — earlier uploaded requirements and feature reports.

Reports may describe proposals, evaluation plans, or prior source checkouts. Check each report’s scope before treating a recommendation or result as a project decision.

